# The goal, versioned in the repo it acts on (ADR-0082).
#
# WHY THIS GOAL EXISTS
#
# Every decomposed goal in this repository has been spent: `triage`, `triage-assist`,
# `moderation-queue`, `support-desk`, `treasury-ledger`, `invoice-copilot`,
# `doc-search-agent` and the archived clinic all have their parts implemented, so
# each one is now refused by the base pre-check (goal 07) rather than run — every
# gate passes against the untouched tree. `docs/CURRENT.md` says a decomposed goal
# has still never been DELIVERED and that the fix for the last two failures is
# "unspent"; the fixes are unspent and the TARGET is not. This is a target.
#
# WHAT MAKES IT DIFFERENT FROM `triage`
#
# Triage's three parts each import a capability the others do not, so a part that
# reimplemented one failed alone, in its own gate. Here `geo:resolve` is imported by
# TWO parts — `schedule` picks the nearest engineer with `distance-meters`, `manifest`
# filters by radius with `bounding-box` and `contains` — so a part that hand-rolls
# haversine can be internally consistent, pass its own gate, and disagree with its
# sibling. The composition gate compares the two numbers directly. That is a failure
# three independent parts can produce and no single part's gate can see, which is the
# thing this goal is for.
#
# `geo:resolve/coords` is also, at the time of writing, imported by nothing in this
# tree — one of the sixteen the contracts test reports as exported-but-unconsumed. So
# a landed run here is additionally the first real consumer of a capability that has
# only ever been checked against itself.
#
# THE LINE ON WHAT THE PARTS ARE TOLD
#
# Same discipline as triage, which learned it the expensive way: a goal specified to
# the depth of its own reference had twelve branches pass on the first attempt at
# score 1000, selection had nothing to choose between, and the search had stopped
# being a search.
#
#   KEPT — facts a part cannot find from what it is shown and that fail SILENTLY:
#          `query` wanting the JSON encoding of a value (it returns `Ok(vec![])` for
#          a wrong query, indistinguishable from an empty collection), `pii::redact`
#          taking `&Options` rather than `Options`, `geo::contains` naming its first
#          parameter `box_`, and `Reply::json` being unable to carry a CSV.
#
#   CUT  — decisions the contract and the imports already imply: which engineer is
#          nearest (arithmetic), that a bounding box is a pre-filter and not the
#          answer (geo.wit says so), what `create_instance` does to an instance that
#          exists (fsm.wit says), and that a transition has to move the document as
#          well as the fsm instance (the contract says the document carries `state`).

text = """
Finish the field-service dispatch API in components/dispatch-domain. Nothing is
implemented yet: three parts are written at the same time, by three agents, against
one contract.

`CONTRACT.md` in that crate is the specification — every route, every status code,
the engineer roster all three parts share, and the shape of the stored document.
Read it first. You may not edit it: if it is wrong or missing something you need,
write CONTRACT-REQUEST.md (first line the subject, the rest why) and the other parts
will answer between generations.

The router in src/lib.rs already exists and is not yours to change. It dispatches to
the three parts, answers /health, seeds a fixture at POST /test/seed, and exposes the
stored document at GET /test/request/{id} so your part can be judged before the part
upstream of it exists. Your part is one file.

You are judged by real HTTP requests against the running component — not by whether
it compiles. `cargo component check` and `cargo component build` both pass happily on
a crate that implements none of its world.

THE POINT OF THIS GOAL: your part's world already contains the hard pieces, as
components, bound at compose time. Look at wit/dispatch.wit. What is imported there
is implemented, tested and composed for you — you call it, you do not write it.
Writing your own PII scanner, your own transition table, your own CSV quoting or your
own haversine is the one way to fail a gate while answering every request correctly,
because the gate reads which capabilities the compiled component actually calls.

Two parts import `geo:resolve` and the composition gate compares the numbers they
produce. A hand-rolled distance formula in either of them is internally consistent,
passes that part's own gate, and disagrees with the other — so it is the whole run
that fails, not your half.

The store, as wit-bindgen generates it in Rust — this is the exact surface, and
guessing it wrong is how earlier runs of other goals died:

    use crate::bindings::records::store::store as records;

    records::create(collection: &str, data: &str, index_fields: &[String]) -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::get(collection: &str, id: &str)                               -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::update(collection: &str, id: &str, data: &str, expected_revision: u64) -> Result<Entry, StoreError>
    records::delete(collection: &str, id: &str)                            -> Result<(), StoreError>
    records::list_records(collection: &str, limit: u32, after: &str)       -> Result<Page, StoreError>
    records::find_by(collection: &str, field: &str, value: &str)           -> Result<Vec<Entry>, StoreError>
    records::query(collection: &str, filters: &[Filter], limit: u32)       -> Result<Vec<Entry>, StoreError>
    records::count(collection: &str)                                       -> Result<u64, StoreError>

    struct Entry  { id: String, data: String, revision: u64, created: u64, … }  // `data` is your JSON
    struct Filter { field: String, value: String }
    struct Page   { entries: Vec<Entry>, next: String }

`query` takes a list of equals-filters, ANDs them, and re-checks each candidate
against every filter — so a two-field list filter and a two-field duplicate lookup
are the same three lines with a different filter vector. `find_by` would need two
calls and a hand-written intersection.

AND `query` AND `find_by` WANT THE JSON ENCODING OF THE VALUE, NOT THE VALUE.
`record-store` indexes the serialised form, so a string field `new` is indexed under
`"new"`, quotes included. A wrong query returns `Ok(vec![])`, which is
indistinguishable from an empty collection — it fails silently, and the gate that
then fails will look like it is about something else.

`find_by` and `query` only filter on a field that was passed in `index_fields` when
the record was created. The router's fixture indexes `state` and `engineer`; anything
else means listing and filtering yourself. The collection is `requests`, and
src/lib.rs's `seed` shows exactly how a request document is written.

There is no wall clock in this world. `created` on the document is RFC3339 UTC
derived from the store's own `created` on the record it just wrote.
"""
title = "A field-service dispatch API, in three parts that share one capability"
contract = "components/dispatch-domain/CONTRACT.md"
writable = []

# WHAT A RUN SHIPS, and it is not the repository.
#
# Without this the run refuses before spending anything: "the base tree is 19.9 MB,
# over the 7.2 MB a run can ship — scope the goal with base_paths to the crate it
# touches (a monorepo cannot ship whole)". This spec had no `base_paths`, no
# `workspace_manifest` and no `keep_members` when it was written, because it was
# written and never run. The `--smoke` pass costs nothing and found it.
#
# The crate under test, plus every capability its world imports — a part cannot
# compose against a WIT it was not shipped.
base_paths = [
  "components/dispatch-domain/",
  "components/record-store/",
  "components/id-generate/",
  # The capabilities these parts compose against.
  "components/pii-redact/",
  "components/fsm-workflow/",
  "components/geo/",
  "components/csv/",
  "components/Cargo.toml",
  "wit/deps/",
  "host/wit/deps/",
  # The guest-side shared code every domain crate depends on by PATH. A spec that
  # keeps a member and not its path dependency ships a workspace cargo cannot load:
  # "failed to load manifest for workspace member". Eight of the nine specs here were
  # missing these — `guestio` and `guestfmt` landed after the goals were written and
  # nothing re-ran them, so nothing said so.
  "components/guestfmt/",
  "components/guestio/",
]
# The workspace is trimmed to these members, so `cargo` in the sandbox does not go
# looking for 200 crates that were not shipped.
workspace_manifest = "components/Cargo.toml"
keep_members = [
  "dispatch-domain",
  "record-store",
  "id-generate",
  "pii-redact",
  "fsm-workflow",
  "geo",
  "csv",
  "guestfmt",
  "guestio",
]

[[part]]
name = "requests"
text = """
Implement components/dispatch-domain/src/requests.rs: taking a service request in,
listing them, and reading one back.

`notes` is stored MASKED, via `pii:redact` — the raw text never reaches the store,
because the manifest is readable by anyone. Empty `kinds` means every kind, which is
what a note typed by a dispatcher needs. Note the reference on `opts`:

    use crate::bindings::pii::redact::redactor as pii;

    pii::redact(text: &str, opts: &pii::Options) -> String

    pub struct Options { pub kinds: Vec<Kind> }   // EMPTY kinds = every kind
    pub enum Kind { Email, CreditCard, Ssn, Phone, Ip }

The duplicate rule is worth reading twice: same `title` AND the same `lat` and `lon`
as an existing request that is NOT `done` and NOT `cancelled` is a 409 naming the
existing id. A finished job does not block a new one — the boiler leaked again.

`invalid` and `bad_coordinate` are different 400s on purpose: a caller can fix only
one of them, and the contract says which is which.
"""
writable = ["components/dispatch-domain/src/requests.rs", "CONTRACT-REQUEST.md"]
context = [
  "components/dispatch-domain/src/lib.rs",
  "components/dispatch-domain/wit/dispatch.wit",
  "components/pii-redact/wit/pii.wit",
  "components/record-store/wit/record.wit",
]
[[part.check]]
id = "requests-part-answers-real-requests"
required = true
weight = 1
command = ["bash", "-c", "cd components && cargo component build --release --target wasm32-wasip2 -p dispatch-domain -p record-store -p id-generate -p pii-redact -p fsm-workflow -p geo -p csv && cargo test --release --manifest-path \"$COMP_GATES/Cargo.toml\" --test gate_dispatch requests_masks_validates_and_deduplicates -- --exact --nocapture"]

[[part]]
name = "schedule"
text = """
Implement components/dispatch-domain/src/schedule.rs: assigning the nearest engineer,
moving a request through its lifecycle, and the queue of what is still open.

THE LIFECYCLE IS A DEFINITION, not a ladder of `if state == "new"`. Register it once
with `fsm:workflow` under the machine name `dispatch`, then drive each request as an
instance whose id is the request id. An illegal move comes back as
`IllegalTransition(String)` carrying the CURRENT state — which is exactly what the
contract's 409 body has to report, so you do not need to look it up yourself.

    use crate::bindings::fsm::workflow::engine as fsm;

    fsm::define(name: &str, def: &fsm::Definition)            -> Result<(), fsm::FsmError>
    fsm::create_instance(machine: &str, instance: &str)       -> Result<fsm::Status, fsm::FsmError>
    fsm::get_status(machine: &str, instance: &str)            -> Result<fsm::Status, fsm::FsmError>
    fsm::fire(machine: &str, instance: &str, event: &str)     -> Result<fsm::Status, fsm::FsmError>

    pub struct Definition { pub states: Vec<String>, pub initial: String,
                            pub transitions: Vec<Transition>, pub terminal: Vec<String> }
    pub struct Transition { pub event: String, pub source: String, pub target: String }
    pub struct Status { pub machine: String, pub instance: String,
                        pub state: String, pub done: bool, pub steps: u32 }

Read `fsm.wit` for how `define` and `create_instance` behave on a store that already
has the machine or the instance — it says, and the difference matters.

THE DISTANCE IS NOT YOURS TO COMPUTE:

    use crate::bindings::geo::resolve::coords as geo;

    geo::distance_meters(a: geo::Point, b: geo::Point) -> Result<f64, geo::GeoError>
    pub struct Point { pub lat: f64, pub lon: f64 }

`manifest` imports the same component to answer its radius filter, and the
composition gate asserts your number equals the one it prints. `distance_m` on the
document is an INTEGER number of metres, rounded half-up.

The fsm instance is not the readable copy: the contract says the document carries
`state`, so a transition moves both.
"""
writable = ["components/dispatch-domain/src/schedule.rs", "CONTRACT-REQUEST.md"]
context = [
  "components/dispatch-domain/src/lib.rs",
  "components/dispatch-domain/wit/dispatch.wit",
  "components/fsm-workflow/wit/fsm.wit",
  "components/geo/wit/geo.wit",
  "components/record-store/wit/record.wit",
]
[[part.check]]
id = "schedule-part-answers-real-requests"
required = true
weight = 1
command = ["bash", "-c", "cd components && cargo component build --release --target wasm32-wasip2 -p dispatch-domain -p record-store -p id-generate -p pii-redact -p fsm-workflow -p geo -p csv && cargo test --release --manifest-path \"$COMP_GATES/Cargo.toml\" --test gate_dispatch schedule_assigns_the_nearest_and_refuses_illegal_moves -- --exact --nocapture"]

[[part]]
name = "manifest"
text = """
Implement components/dispatch-domain/src/manifest.rs: the day's work as a JSON
summary and as a CSV, both with an optional radius filter.

The counting is yours; the CSV is not. Use `csv:codec`'s `format`, because one seeded
request is titled `Boiler leaking, badly` and a field with a comma in it has to come
back quoted or the row stops having five columns. The gate parses the CSV with a real
parser and counts columns, and it also reads the compiled component's imports — so
formatting it by hand fails even if you get the quoting right.

    use crate::bindings::csv::codec::codec as csv;

    pub struct Row     { pub fields: Vec<String> }
    pub struct Dialect { pub delimiter: String,   // a STRING, not a char: ",".to_string()
                         pub has_header: bool, pub trim: bool }
    csv::format(rows: &[Row], opts: &Dialect) -> String

A file is `Vec<Row>` — the header is just the first `Row` — and `format` returns the
whole document as one `String`.

The gate checks the content type as well as the columns, so look at what `Reply` in
src/lib.rs can actually answer with before you build the response.

THE RADIUS IS NOT YOURS TO COMPUTE:

    use crate::bindings::geo::resolve::coords as geo;

    geo::bounding_box(center: geo::Point, radius_meters: f64) -> Result<geo::Bbox, geo::GeoError>
    geo::contains(box_: geo::Bbox, p: geo::Point)             -> bool
    geo::distance_meters(a: geo::Point, b: geo::Point)        -> Result<f64, geo::GeoError>

`schedule` imports the same component to pick an engineer, and the composition gate
compares what the two of you produce.

`by_state` and `by_engineer` include only the states and engineers that OCCUR: no
zero-filled keys, and an unassigned request contributes to no engineer at all — `""`
is not a key. A day with nothing in it is a manifest of zero, not a 404.
"""
writable = ["components/dispatch-domain/src/manifest.rs", "CONTRACT-REQUEST.md"]
context = [
  "components/dispatch-domain/src/lib.rs",
  "components/dispatch-domain/wit/dispatch.wit",
  "components/csv/wit/csv.wit",
  "components/geo/wit/geo.wit",
  "components/record-store/wit/record.wit",
]
[[part.check]]
id = "manifest-part-answers-real-requests"
required = true
weight = 1
command = ["bash", "-c", "cd components && cargo component build --release --target wasm32-wasip2 -p dispatch-domain -p record-store -p id-generate -p pii-redact -p fsm-workflow -p geo -p csv && cargo test --release --manifest-path \"$COMP_GATES/Cargo.toml\" --test gate_dispatch manifest_counts_quotes_and_filters_by_radius -- --exact --nocapture"]

# The COMPOSITION gate: the one no single part can pass. It drives one request the
# whole way through all three — `requests` takes it in and masks it, `schedule`
# assigns it and moves it, `manifest` reports it — and then compares the distance
# `schedule` computed with the one `manifest` printed, and checks that the request
# `schedule` placed falls inside a radius `manifest` measured. A part that stored its
# own shape, or hand-rolled its own haversine, passes its own gate and fails here.
[[check]]
id = "the-whole-dispatch-api-works"
required = true
weight = 1
command = ["bash", "-c", "cd components && cargo component build --release --target wasm32-wasip2 -p dispatch-domain -p record-store -p id-generate -p pii-redact -p fsm-workflow -p geo -p csv && cargo test --release --manifest-path \"$COMP_GATES/Cargo.toml\" --test gate_dispatch the_whole_dispatch_api_works -- --exact --nocapture"]
